{"id": "u5a9bbe0dde", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41556-026-02119-z", "source": "Nature Cell Biology", "title": "Endolysosomal damage promotes intraluminal protein condensate formation that limits cytosolic leakage - Nature Cell Biology", "byline": "Li; Xu; Zhang; Yu; Zhao; Kaiqiang; Chen; Cui; Di; Nikan; Mehran; Young; Stephen G; Xiaoyu; Bennett; C Frank; Seth; Punit P; Tao; Gutierrez; Maximiliano G; Jiang", "date": "Nov 20", "image": "https://media.springernature.com/m685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41556-026-02119-z/MediaObjects/41556_2026_2119_Fig1_HTML.png", "thumb": "5a9bbe0dde.jpg", "html": "<h2>Abstract</h2>\n    <p>Endolysosomal membrane damage is a detrimental process in mammalian cells that results in leakage of the luminal contents into the cytosol. However, the nature and extent of the leakage during membrane damage are unknown. Here we show that endomembrane damage induces the rapid formation of intraluminal condensates in endolysosomes. A subset of resident luminal proteins undergoes spatially coordinated condensation on endomembrane damage. Electron microscopy reveals distinct luminal morphology, and cryo-electron tomography confirms the compact ultrastructure in their native state. Condensate formation occurs across mechanistically distinct modes of membrane injury, and the condensates are reversibly dissociated on lysosomal recovery. Remarkably, these condensates impose a barrier to endolysosomal escape of therapeutic oligonucleotides. Despite endomembrane damage, luminal oligonucleotide therapeutics are sequestered in damaged endolysosomes through condensate-mediated biophysical immobilization. Targeting condensate sequestration could represent a strategy to improve oligonucleotide-based therapeutics.</p>\n    <h2>Access options</h2>\n    <p>Access Nature and 54 other Nature Portfolio journals</p>\n    <p>Get Nature+, our best-value online-access subscription</p>\n    <p>Subscribe to this journal</p>\n    <p>Receive 12 print issues and online access</p>\n    <p>Buy this article</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Purchase on SpringerLink</li>\n      <li>Instant access to the full article PDF.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p>USD 39.95</p>\n    <p>Prices may be subject to local taxes which are calculated during checkout</p>\n    <img src=\"https://media.springernature.com/m312/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41556-026-02119-z/MediaObjects/41556_2026_2119_Fig1_HTML.png\" alt=\"This image shows how the fluorescence intensity and localization of specific markers change over time in response to treatments with LLOMe and GPN, indicating lysosomal membrane permeabilization and redistribution of luminal contents. a and c show time series of cells labeled with LAMP1 (a lysosomal membrane marker) and dextran (a lysosomal lumen marker) before and after treatment with LLOMe (a) or GPN (c). Over time, dextran fluorescence intensity increases and redistributes, indicating spatial reorganisation upon lysosomal membrane permeabilization. The 3D surface plots below each time point illustrate the changes in dextran fluorescence intensity distribution. b and d present graphs of relative fluorescence intensity over time after LLOMe (b) or GPN (d) treatment. Both mean and maximum fluorescence intensities increase, with maximum intensity rising more sharply, reflecting lysosomal disruption dynamics. e shows colocalization of LAMP1 and EGF-647 fluorescence signals, with a line profile plot demonstrating overlap in intensity peaks, indicating EGF localization within lysosomes. f quantifies the overlap fraction between EGF and LAMP1 signals, showing a high degree of colocalization. g and i display EGF-647 fluorescence before and after LLOMe (g) or GPN (i) treatment, with insets highlighting individual lysosomes. Post-treatment images show increased maximum fluorescence intensity and altered distribution. h and j plot maximum EGF fluorescence intensity before and after LLOMe (h) or GPN (j) treatment, showing significant increases after treatment. k and l show cathepsin D localization and maximum fluorescence intensity before and after treatment with LLOMe combined with the inhibitor E64d. 3D surface plots illustrate changes in cathepsin D distribution, with increased maximum intensity post-treatment. m summarizes maximum cathepsin D fluorescence intensity normalized to baseline across conditions, showing significant increases with LLOMe treatment and no increase when combined with E64d. Overall, the panels demonstrate that LLOMe and GPN treatments that inducing lysosomal membrane permeabilization lead to increased fluorescence intensity of luminal cargos or markers, with quantification supporting these observations.\" loading=\"lazy\">\n    <img src=\"https://media.springernature.com/m312/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41556-026-02119-z/MediaObjects/41556_2026_2119_Fig2_HTML.png\" alt=\"This figure presents experimental data on the interaction between Proteostat and GAL-8 proteins in cells treated with different substances. Panel a shows cell images with Proteostat puncta increasing after treatment with LLOMe, GPN, and silica compared to basal conditions. Panel b quantifies the normalized mean fluorescence intensity of Proteostat, showing significant increases after LLOMe, GPN, and silica treatments compared to basal. Panel c displays frequency distributions of normalized proteostat intensity for basal, LLOMe, GPN, and silica conditions, indicating shifts toward higher intensities with treatments. Panel d shows cell images stained for Proteostat and GAL-8, with increased colocalization after 5 minutes of LLOMe treatment; an inset graph plots normalized intensity profiles of Proteostat and GAL-8 along a line, showing overlapping peaks. Panels e and f quantify the number of Proteostat and GAL-8 puncta per cell, respectively, with significant increases after LLOMe treatment. Panel g shows the fraction of Proteostat puncta that are positive or negative for GAL-8, with most puncta positive after LLOMe treatment. Panel h outlines the experimental workflow for cell treatment, lysis, and immunoprecipitation to separate soluble and insoluble fractions. Panel i presents western blot results for lysosomal proteins LAMP1, mature CTSB, mature CTSD, GBA, TPP1, and PSAP in soluble and insoluble fractions from whole-cell lysates and anti-HA immunoprecipitates, comparing mock and LLOMe-treated cells.\" loading=\"lazy\">\n    <img src=\"https://media.springernature.com/m312/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41556-026-02119-z/MediaObjects/41556_2026_2119_Fig3_HTML.png\" alt=\"This figure compares cellular structures under three conditions: untreated (Mock), treated with LLOMe, and treated with GPN. Panels a, b, and c show electron microscopy images combined with fluorescence labeling of LAMP1 and nuclei, highlighting lysosomal structures marked by correlative light and electron microscopy (CLEM). Enlarged views focus on lysosomes indicated by arrowheads. Panels d, h, and m present electron tomography slices showing lysosomal morphology, with condensates outlined in treated cells. Panels e, i, and n provide 3D reconstructions of lysosomes, illustrating membrane boundaries and internal condensates or structures. Panels f, j, k, l, o, and p show detailed views of lysosomal membranes and internal condensates, with electron tomography slices and corresponding 3D renderings. The images collectively demonstrate changes in lysosomal morphology and internal condensate formation following LLOMe and GPN treatments compared to the untreated control.\" loading=\"lazy\">\n    <img src=\"https://media.springernature.com/m312/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41556-026-02119-z/MediaObjects/41556_2026_2119_Fig4_HTML.png\" alt=\"This figure presents experimental data on the effects of LLOMe treatment on lysosomal membrane permeabilization, pH changes, and proteostasis markers in cells. Panel a illustrates that LLOMe treatment increases lysosomal membrane permeabilization and raises lysosomal pH. Panels b through d show that after 3 minutes of LLOMe treatment, lysosomes labeled with LysoTracker exhibits decreased fluorescence intensity, indicating altered lysosomal pH; while loaded dextran exhibits increased maximal fluoresence intensity indicating intraluminal condensate formation. Panels e through j compare cells treated with Bafilomycin A1 or CQ for 24 hours, showing the majority of raised Proteostat signal are GAL-8 negative indicating loss of acidic pH can induce the formation of protein complex condensates. Panels k through m display images and quantifications of proteostat and LAMP1 colocalization under different conditions, including LLOMe treatment and chase periods, demonstrating changes in endolysosomal proteostasis. Panels n through q provide electron microscopy images and quantifications of GAL-8 and Magic Red puncta per cell under baseline, LLOMe treatment, and chase conditions, showing significant increases in puncta number after LLOMe treatment and recovery after chase without Bafilomycin A1. However, chase with Bafilomycin A1 blocks lysosome reacidification and caused persistent intraluminal condensates and Gal-8 puncta, and cathepsin activity failed to recover. Statistical significance is indicated for comparisons between conditions.\" loading=\"lazy\">\n    <img src=\"https://media.springernature.com/m312/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41556-026-02119-z/MediaObjects/41556_2026_2119_Fig5_HTML.png\" alt=\"This figure presents experimental data on the effects of LLOMe treatment on MALAT1 mRNA expression, intracellular localization of antisense oligonucleotides (ASO), and proteostat staining in cells. Panels a and b show quantitative measurements of MALAT1 mRNA expression levels, as an indicative of ASO activity. Panel a compares expression in mock-treated cells and cells treated with LLOMe for various times, showing a significant decrease in MALAT1 expression after 1- and 3-minute of LLOMe treatment, with no further significant change at later times. Panel b shows dose-response curves of MALAT1 expression relative to ASO concentration in mock and LLOMe-treated cells, indicating a stronger reduction in MALAT1 expression with increasing ASO concentration in LLOMe-treated cells. Panel c displays fluorescence microscopy images of cells before and after 3 minutes of LLOMe treatment, showing ASO and LTR signals. After treatment, ASO signal appears more punctate and colocalizes with weaker LTR signal in intracellular compartments. Panel d quantifies mean fluorescence intensity of LTR signal per cell masked by ASO signal, showing a significant decrease after 1 and 3 minutes of LLOMe treatment. Panel e quantifies mean fluorescence intensity of luminal ASO per cell, showing no significant change after LLOMe treatment. Panel f shows a time series of fluorescence images tracking proteostat staining in cells after LLOMe treatment, revealing ASO are remained within endolysosomes during prolonged LLOMe treatment. Panel g presents time-lapse images of cells before and after LLOMe treatment, showing ASO and GAL-8 signals. ASO signal appears to accumulate and colocalize with GAL-8 over time after treatment. Panel h graphs normalized mean intensity of GAL-8 and normalised maximal intensity of ASO signals over time after LLOMe treatment, showing GAL-8 intensity increases rapidly upon LLOMe treatment and temporally coupled with the increased maximal ASO fluoresence intensity. Panel i is a schematic of the experimental workflow for correlative light and electron microscopy (CLEM) analysis of ASO uptake, fixation, proteostat staining, and imaging. Panel j shows fluorescence and electron microscopy images of cells stained for nucleus, proteostat, and ASO after LLOMe treatment. Insets highlight three regions showing ASO and proteostat localization relative to cellular ultrastructure. Panel k quantifies mean fluorescence intensity of proteostat staining in three cellular compartments: assembly, cavity, and cytosol. Proteostat intensity is significantly higher in assembly compared to cavity compartments and cytosol. Panel l shows fluorescence recovery after photobleaching (FRAP) time series images of proteostat signal in mock and LLOMe-treated cells, indicating no clearer recovery in LLOMe-treated cells. Panel m graphs normalized proteostat fluorescence intensity over time after photobleaching, showing no recovery in LLOMe-treated cells compared to mock. Overall, the figure demonstrates that induced endolysosomal damage can not completely release luminal ASOs and promote ASO sequestration in intracellular compartments associated with proteostat-positive assemblies.\" loading=\"lazy\">\n    <h3>Similar content being viewed by others</h3>\n    <img src=\"https://media.springernature.com/w215h120/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs42003-022-03132-2/MediaObjects/42003_2022_3132_Fig1_HTML.png\" loading=\"lazy\">\n    <img src=\"https://media.springernature.com/w215h120/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41586-023-06726-w/MediaObjects/41586_2023_6726_Fig1_HTML.png\" loading=\"lazy\">\n    <img src=\"https://media.springernature.com/w215h120/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41589-024-01717-y/MediaObjects/41589_2024_1717_Figa_HTML.png\" loading=\"lazy\">\n    <h2>Data availability</h2>\n    <p>The cryo-electron tomograms generated in this study have been deposited in the Electron Microscopy Data Bank (EMDB). The tomograms of endolysosomes in mock-treated U2OS cells are available under accessions EMD-83878 (Fig. 3d,g), EMD-83888 (Extended Data Fig. 5d, top) and EMD-83889 (Extended Data Fig. 5d, bottom). The tomograms of endolysosomes in LLOMe-treated U2OS cells are available under accessions EMD-83886 (Fig. 3h,k), EMD-83890 (Extended Data Fig. 5e, top) and EMD-83891 (Extended Data Fig. 5e, bottom). The tomograms of endolysosomes in GPN-treated U2OS cells are available under accessions EMD-83887 (Fig. 3m,p), EMD-83868 (Extended Data Fig. 5f, top) and EMD-83869 (Extended Data Fig. 5f, bottom). The data needed to evaluate the conclusions of the study are available in this Article. Other data supporting the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. Source data are provided with this paper.</p>\n    <h2>References</h2>\n    <p class=\"trimmed\">…</p>", "word_count": 362, "status": "full", "reason": null, "summary": "Li et al. show that intraluminal condensates form quickly upon endolysosome damage and that condensates sequester luminal antisense oligonucleotides, suggesting a mechanism preventing the escape of antisense…", "extracted_at": "2026-11-23T05:37:37-0500", "http": "200"}